## The Ghost Property

The Labs subnet is invisible in three distinct ways:

### 1 — Invisible to the Internet

```
Internet sees:     YOUR_PUBLIC_IP:443 only
Internet does not see:
    → YOUR_MIDDLE_SUBNET (behind NAT #1)
    → YOUR_LABS_SUBNET (behind NAT #2)
    → Any device on Labs
    → Any service on Labs
    → The server itself
```

No inbound connection to Labs is possible from the internet. NAT without port forwards is not a firewall — it is a one-way door. Traffic flows out. Nothing gets in uninvited.

### 2 — Invisible to the Home Network

```
Home devices see:   Edge router at YOUR_HOME_SUBNET.1 only
Home does not see:
    → Middle router (different subnet, no route advertised)
    → Server (behind middle router NAT)
    → Labs devices (behind server routing)
```

A phone on the home Wi-Fi cannot ping, scan, or reach any Labs device. They are on different subnets with no route between them.

### 3 — Invisible Even to Each Other (Partially)

```
Labs Desktop sees:  Server at YOUR_LABS_SUBNET.1 (its gateway)
Labs Desktop does not know:
    → What is above the server
    → That a middle router exists
    → What subnet the server's uplink is on
    → The edge router's existence
```

Each layer only knows its immediate neighbors.

### The Kill Switch

If the server’s wireless uplink to the middle router drops for any reason:

```
wlan0 goes down
    ↓
nftables forward chain has no matching rule for eth0 → anything else
    ↓
All Labs outbound traffic is dropped immediately
    ↓
Labs go completely dark
    ↓
No route exists — traffic cannot leak anywhere
    ↓
Human intervention required to restore
```

This is not a firewall rule. It is the absence of a route. Nothing can route around it.

