## The Kill Switch

The kill switch is implemented in `nftables` on the server. It is not a firewall rule — it is a routing architecture that makes leaks structurally impossible.

```bash
# /etc/nftables.d/homelab.nft

table inet homelab {
    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;

        # Allow established/related back to Labs
        ct state established,related accept

        # Labs → Internet via wlan0 ONLY
        # If wlan0 is down this rule never matches
        # Labs traffic drops — no other path exists
        iifname "eth0" oifname "wlan0" accept

        # Return traffic to Labs
        iifname "wlan0" oifname "eth0" ct state established,related accept
    }

    chain postrouting {
        type nat hook postrouting priority 100;
        # Masquerade Labs traffic as server's IP
        iifname "eth0" oifname "wlan0" masquerade
    }
}
```

**Why this is a kill switch and not just a firewall rule:**

A firewall rule can be bypassed by a sufficiently privileged process. A missing route cannot. When `wlan0` goes down:

```
eth0 → wlan0 forward rule exists but interface is down
    → rule never matches
    → default policy is DROP
    → Labs traffic is dropped at the kernel level
    → No process, no privilege escalation, no container escape
      can create a route that doesn't exist
```

